#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""moses-drift — compare what Moses DECLARES about himself against what is actually running.

    moses-drift            report only disagreements; silence means they agree
    moses-drift --all      show every check, agreeing or not

WHY THIS EXISTS. Brad, 2026-08-22: "I just don't get why we're still finding things so very wrong
with Moses." Nothing was wrong with the mechanisms. Every surprise that day was a DECLARATION that
nothing compared against reality, and then quoted as fact:

  * the roster said Knight was "parked - needs a model" — he had been pushing to master since Aug 8
  * memory named the roster /etc/moses/personas.json — that path has never existed
  * the roster in the source tree was three weeks stale and had no Therapist in it
  * mcp_server.py in the source tree served 16 tools; the running server served 34
  * TWO Moses listeners answered Slack for six days, one running week-old code

`project_status` was built on the right principle — the DISAGREEMENT between declared and actual is
the signal — and nothing applied it to the roster or the infrastructure. This does.

TWO RULES IT KEEPS ABOUT ITSELF:
  1. A check that could not run says so, and is never counted as agreement. "Couldn't look" and
     "looked and found nothing" are different facts; collapsing them is the failure this whole day
     was made of.
  2. It reports disagreements only. A wall of green is a wall nobody reads, and the finding gets
     lost in it.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

